Revo OS
=======

Runs on [kernel.zig](https://github.com/xt9y/kernel.zig), a standalone x86-64
Zig kernel. Uses Zig 0.17.0, Revo, Limine and QEMU.

## Build and run

On macOS, install Zig, QEMU and xorriso:

    brew install zig qemu xorriso

Initialize git submodules:

    git submodule update --init --recursive

Then:

    zig build          # Compile kernel ELF
    zig build iso      # Bootable ISO
    zig build run      # Serial-only terminal
    zig build run-gui  # QEMU VGA window; Revo can draw into its framebuffer

The kernel defaults to ReleaseSmall. Zig build options are available:

    zig build run-gui -Dmax-tasks=16 -Dprocess-code-pages=8 -Dheap-mib=64

## Design boundary

The kernel and Zig bridge provide hardware access ONLY.

Graphics, text rendering, glyphs, layout, compositor, GUI shell, input handling,
window management and applications are intentionally NOT implemented by Zig.
Implement all those behaviors in your own src/*.rv files.

The original src/main.rv, src/cmd.rv and src/fs.rv are unchanged by this branch.
Thus run-gui initially opens a framebuffer without drawing a Revo GUI.
The serial shell still works. The Revo application must opt into the
callbacks documented below to display its text and process its keyboard.

## Framebuffer host API (Revo)

    fb_width() -> number             # 0 if unavailable
    fb_height() -> number            # 0 if unavailable
    fb_clear(rgb) -> bool            # 0xRRGGBB, passed as a numeric value
    fb_pixel(x, y, rgb) -> bool
    fb_rect(x, y, width, height, rgb) -> bool

    fb_blit_rgb(x, y, w, h, hex) -> bool

All coordinates and dimensions must be non-negative integral numbers.
RGB is a numeric value 0 through 16777215 (0xFFFFFF). Rectangle drawing
clips at framebuffer boundaries. Invalid framebuffer / color returns false.

fb_blit_rgb takes a Revo string of 6 hexadecimal characters per RGB888
pixel, row-major top-to-bottom and left-to-right, e.g. "ff000000ff00"
for red then green. The entire tile must fit in the framebuffer. Up to
65536 pixels may be sent in one call; use multiple tiles for larger images.
This is a byte transfer primitive, not a glyph, font, image loader or UI API.

## Input/output hooks (optional Revo globals)

    global on_scancode = fn(byte) do
        # byte: 0..255 raw PS/2 keyboard scan-code stream.
        # Includes make/break bytes and E0/E1 prefixes.
        # Implement keyboard layout, modifiers and text dispatch in Revo.
    end

    global on_output = fn(text) do
        # A batch of output bytes from serial_puts() or print().
        # You choose whether/how to draw them in your own terminal.
    end

The bridge invokes the hooks only when present. Existing serial shell input
continues to use global on_input(ch) and is not automatically reinterpreted
as a PS/2 key. on_output is called after interpreter callbacks to avoid
re-entering the VM from a native host function. It also receives output
produced during Revo startup, when the callback is defined before boot ends.

The QEMU PC PS/2 controller is expected to produce scan-code set 1 as
delivered by the BIOS controller translation. Mapping keys to characters,
Shift, AltGr, Caps Lock and international layouts is completely your job
in Revo. Mouse events are not part of this patch.

## Existing host functions

    serial_puts(text)       # COM1, plus optional on_output mirroring
    time_ms()
    disk_sectors()
    disk_read_hex(lba)
    disk_write_hex(lba, hex)
    proc_count()
    proc_state(pid)
    proc_spawn_hex(code)

This is not an ELF loader: ring-3 processes currently accept raw
position-independent x86-64 machine code. An on-device Zig compiler and
separate GUI application IPC are not yet implemented.
